# ─── Anthers — local dev configuration ───
# cp .env.example .env, then fill in the three DEV_ACCOUNT_ lines. That's it.
#
# THIS FILE IS DEV-ONLY. `.env` is gitignored and excluded from the Docker build
# context, and App Platform injects its own values, so nothing here reaches prod.
#
# It is short because almost nothing needs to be here:
#   • Secrets come from the "Anthers Dev" Bitwarden project — `make dev` injects
#     them and they never touch disk.
#   • Non-secret config that dev shares with prod is read from the committed
#     `.do/app.yaml` at boot (see apps/api/src/dev-spec-env.ts).
#   • Everything else already has a working dev default in the code.


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Required
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# The containerized Postgres from compose.yaml, which `make dev` starts. The line
# below is already correct — keep it, don't change it.
#
# ⚠️ This one cannot be dropped in favour of the code default, even though two of
# its three readers have one. `packages/db/src/migrate.ts` REQUIRES it and exits,
# deliberately: a migration runner that silently falls back to a default database
# is how you migrate the wrong one. `make db-ready` fails without this.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://anthers:anthers@localhost:5432/anthers

# `make dev` ensures a local account from these, so a wiped dev DB never costs
# you your login. Use the same username/email/password as your prod account.
# Leave blank to skip the bootstrap entirely.
#
# USERNAME is the match key, so changing EMAIL moves the address on your existing
# account rather than creating a second one. PASSWORD is never rewritten on an
# account that already exists — a mismatch is reported, not applied.
# ⚠️ Bun's .env parser expands `$` even inside single quotes; escape each as `\$`.
DEV_ACCOUNT_USERNAME=
DEV_ACCOUNT_EMAIL=
DEV_ACCOUNT_PASSWORD=


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Optional — every one of these already defaults correctly for dev
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Uncomment only to override. The value shown is what the code uses when unset.

# local = the filesystem under ./content. s3 = Cloudflare R2, what prod runs.
# On `local` no other STORAGE_* var is read at all. To point dev at real R2 you
# need the full set from .do/app.yaml plus a key pair, and STORAGE_REGION must
# be `auto` — R2 validates the name, and a region string fails every call.
#STORAGE_BACKEND=local

# Where the API sends users for links in email, and the origin CORS allows.
#FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Session-cookie domain. Unset = host-only, correct when the SPA and API share
# an origin, as they do locally. Prod sets `.anthers.org` so www works.
#COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# Sender for transactional email. Override if your dev Resend key can't send
# from a verified anthers.org address.
#EMAIL_FROM=Anthers <noreply@anthers.org>

# false → the bootstrap account is a plain consumer rather than a creator.
#DEV_ACCOUNT_CREATOR=true

# true → also grants /admin, which is the moderation queue. For an account this
# file doesn't manage, or one in prod: `bun run db:admin <username>`.
#DEV_ACCOUNT_ADMIN=false

# A test-mode acct_… linked to the gauntlet's fixture creator, so its purchase
# rung can complete a real test-mode checkout. Unset = that rung is skipped,
# which is how CI runs it. Needed again after any DB rebuild.
#GAUNTLET_STRIPE_ACCOUNT=


# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Secrets — normally not here at all
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# `make dev` injects these from the "Anthers Dev" Bitwarden project. You only
# need them below for `make dev-local`, which is the offline path.
#
# 🚨 "Anthers Dev" is a SEPARATE project from "Anthers", which holds production.
# The split is the point: a leaked `.env` or a compromised laptop must not be a
# production incident. If you fill these in, use dev values — never prod's.
#
#   SITE_PASSWORD           the pre-launch gate. Anything; it guards localhost.
#                           ⚠️ Empty does not disable the gate, it seals it.
#   SITE_ACCESS_KEYS        comma-separated keys for /?invite=<key>, which skips
#                           the gate. One per audience, so a link can be revoked.
#   STRIPE_SECRET_KEY       a restricted test key (rk_test_) from the sandbox
#                           account. Permissions: write on PaymentIntents,
#                           Refunds, Customers, Subscriptions, Customer portal,
#                           Connect Accounts, Connect Account links, Transfers;
#                           read on PaymentMethods; none on everything else.
#   STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET   printed by `stripe listen` (make stripe-webhooks).
#   RESEND_API_KEY          resend.com → API keys. Sending access is enough.
